from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P197-0001','orbit transport equivalence omitted','Proposition 5.1.2 setup: pointwise equivariant modules','Compatible semilinear modules on each orbit are equivalent to modules over the stabilizer skew rings at chosen orbit representatives.','Choose transporters from representatives to points, impose the stabilizer relation when changing transporter, and construct the inverse equivalence. State which identifications depend on choices and which Hom descriptions are canonical.','stabilizer|skew group ring|orbit representative')
add('P197-0002','coinduction and Hom adjunction calculation','Proposition 5.1.2: equivariant product injectives','Equivariant maps into P(A) correspond to stabilizer-module maps from the representative stalks; injective factors make P(A) injective.','Use coinduction, described by equivariant functions on G and a product over cosets, to transport each representative factor. Prove the product Hom adjunction and exactness of the representative-stalk functor. The historical term induced here means the Hom/product, or coinduced, construction.','coinduction|injective equivariant sheaf|stabilizer')
add('P197-0003','equivariance verification omitted','§5.1: equivariant direct image','Direct image to the orbit space inherits a group action on sections over inverse-image opens.','Every inverse-image open is invariant, so the given action sends its sections to its sections and commutes with restriction. Verify preservation of the additional group, ring or module operations.','equivariant direct image|orbit space')
add('P198-0001','invariants and scalar-action verification','§5.1: invariant direct image','Invariant sections form a sheaf and a left-exact functor, with the appropriate module structure over invariant ring sections.','Fixed-point conditions are local and compatible with gluing; verify the scalar action uses invariant scalars and that kernels commute with fixed points. Distinguish the ordinary direct image, with its G-action, from its invariant subsheaf.','invariant direct image|left exact|ring invariants')
add('P198-0002','injectivity of coinduction used','Proposition 5.1.3: direct image of an injective G-sheaf','Equivariant direct image preserves injectivity in the stated abelian sheaf categories.','Express an injective as a direct summand of a representative-stalk product; coinduction is right adjoint to exact restriction of group modules and therefore preserves injectives. Then apply the product-sheaf lifting criterion on the orbit space.','coinduction|direct image|injective G-sheaf')
add('P198-0003','acyclicity corollary compressed','Corollary to Proposition 5.1.3','The section and invariant-direct-image functors have the acyclicity properties needed for the two composite spectral sequences.','Use the equality of global sections before and after direct image, the injectivity result just proved, and the earlier group-module Hom/flasque acyclicity statements. Verify each target category rather than substituting ordinary injectivity for equivariant injectivity.','equivariant cohomology|acyclicity|composite functor')
add('P198-0004','unit isomorphism verification omitted','§5.1, equation (5.1.1): descent unit','A sheaf on the quotient is recovered as invariant direct image of its inverse image.','Describe inverse-image sections locally as pullbacks of base sections. An invariant section is constant along each orbit in the etale-space sense and descends continuously because the orbit map is open and quotient. Verify the resulting local maps glue and give the inverse to the unit.','descent|quotient map|inverse image unit')
add('P199-0001','essential-image criterion and local converse omitted','§5.1, equation (5.1.2): descent counit','The invariant descent counit is monic; under condition (D), trivial stabilizer action on every stalk characterizes descent from the quotient.','For monicity use the invariant equalizer locus of two local sections and openness of its image. Under (D) choose a stabilizer-stable slice and average the local compatibility by shrinking over the finite stabilizer, then extend over its translates. In the free case this yields the equivalence of sheaf categories. Keep the separate footnote correction: a general equivariant map is not thereby monic.','descent counit|stabilizer triviality|condition D')
add('P199-0002','examples asserted, some by definition','§5.1: invariant function sheaves','Trivially acted constant sheaves descend to the same constant sheaf on the quotient; invariant function sheaves describe quotient functions under the specified structure and hypotheses.','Use the quotient-map criterion for locally constant or continuous functions. For smooth, analytic or algebraic functions state the chosen quotient structure and verify local invariant sections in that structure; the phrase by definition cannot establish existence of a quotient of the advertised category.','invariant functions|quotient structure|constant sheaf')
add('P200-0001','restriction of injectives omitted','§5.2: locality of equivariant derived functors','Restriction of an injective G-sheaf to the inverse image of an open subset of the quotient remains injective.','Use the exact equivariant extension-by-zero functor left adjoint to that restriction; exactness follows on underlying stalks. Derive the local invariant-direct-image formula using this preserved injective resolution.','equivariant restriction|extension by zero|derived invariants')
add('P200-0002','slice equivalence asserted','Corollary to Proposition 5.2.1: induction from a subgroup slice','For a disjoint union of translates of a slice, equivariant sheaves are equivalent to sheaves on the slice equivariant for its stabilizing subgroup.','Restrict to the slice, reconstruct by transporting over cosets, and check the two functors are inverse up to natural isomorphism and preserve invariant sections. Exactness then identifies their derived functors.','slice|induction equivalence|stabilizer')
add('P201-0001','edge map from a third spectral sequence','§5.2, equation (5.2.10): ordinary direct-image comparison','The third spectral sequence supplies a natural map from quotient-side equivariant cohomology of f_*A to equivariant cohomology of A.','Apply the composite-functor theorem with the established injective-image property and identify the q=0 edge morphism. The author’s qualitative description as pathological is not a separate mathematical claim or a substitute for specifying when higher direct images vanish.','equivariant Leray|edge map|higher direct image')
add('P202-0001','comparison of underlying derived functors','Proposition 5.2.2: ordinary higher direct images','For an equivariant sheaf, the underlying sheaves of the derived ordinary direct images are the ordinary higher direct-image sheaves.','Use equivariant injective resolutions whose underlying sheaves are flasque, prove they are acyclic on inverse images of opens, and sheafify the resulting local cohomology presheaves. Equivariant injectivity alone needs the forgetful/acyclicity justification.','higher direct image|equivariant injective|flasque')
add('P202-0002','spectral-sequence collapse and applications','Proposition 5.2.3: comparison with quotient cohomology','Vanishing of positive derived invariant-direct-image sheaves makes equivariant cohomology equal to cohomology of the quotient invariant sheaf.','Collapse the first spectral sequence of Theorem 5.2.1 and identify its edge isomorphism. The printed reference to 2.5.1 is corrected. For finite stabilizers with invertible orders, prove group-cohomology vanishing by the averaging homotopy before using the collapse.','quotient cohomology|averaging|spectral sequence collapse')
add('P203-0001','informal frequency observation','Proposition 5.2.2, Remark 3: scope comment','The claim that the hypotheses occur in most natural cases is qualitative commentary, not a precise theorem.','No mathematical proof obligation attaches to the frequency wording. Each subsequent concrete example must independently satisfy the stated hypotheses.','higher direct image|finite group')
add('P203-0002','algebraic applications stated without proof','§5.2, equation (5.2.11 bis) and algebraic examples','Finite group actions allow the stated stalk calculation; the listed coherent-sheaf and unit-sheaf applications require the algebraic quotient and unramified hypotheses.','For finite groups use a finite free bar resolution in each degree to commute group cohomology with filtered stalk limits. For coherent sheaves use finite/affine quotient calculations; for unramified finite torsors use faithfully flat descent. For units, separate the degree-one Hilbert-90/Picard argument from arbitrary higher vanishing. Do not identify Zariski and etale topology or drop the stated quotient-existence condition.','finite quotient|coherent sheaf|descent|Hilbert 90')
add('P203-0003','slice shrinking and examples omitted','§5.3, condition (D): finite stabilizer neighborhoods','A condition-(D) neighborhood can be made open and stabilizer-invariant; finite actions on Hausdorff spaces satisfy (D).','Intersect the finitely many stabilizer translates after choosing a suitable open neighborhood. For a finite Hausdorff action separate x from the finitely many other orbit points and shrink so unwanted translates are disjoint. The word separated in this topological claim means Hausdorff.','condition D|finite stabilizer|Hausdorff')
add('P204-0001','irreducible-space observation omitted','§5.3: failure of (D) for nontrivial finite Zariski actions','On an irreducible Zariski space, a nontrivial action with a moved point cannot satisfy the disjoint-neighborhood part of (D).','Two nonempty open subsets of an irreducible space always intersect. Apply this to a neighborhood of a moved point and its translate; the group element is outside that point’s stabilizer.','irreducible|Zariski|condition D')
add('P204-0002','theorem proved by local reduction','Theorem 5.3.1: stalks of equivariant cohomology sheaves','Under (D), the stalk of the equivariant cohomology sheaf at an orbit is the cohomology of the finite stabilizer with coefficients in the corresponding stalk.','Use the slice equivalence to reduce to the finite stabilizer, prove vanishing of ordinary higher direct images locally, and commute the finite-group cochain construction with the stalk limit. Record the canonical identifications under change of orbit representative.','stabilizer cohomology|condition D|equivariant stalk')
add('P204-0003','local vanishing calculation within proof','Theorem 5.3.1: direct-image acyclicity step','After the preceding reduction to a finite group, local inverse images split into finitely many translates and their positive cohomology vanishes in the stalk limit.','Use cofinal invariant neighborhoods for the finite stabilizer, finite disjoint-union cohomology, and local vanishing of positive cohomology classes. The finite-sum calculation occurs after reduction; it is not a claim that an arbitrary original orbit is finite.','finite slice|higher direct image|local vanishing')
add('P205-0001','free-action consequence','§5.3: fixed-point-free case','Under (D) and trivial stabilizers, equivariant cohomology equals cohomology of the quotient descended sheaf.','Apply Theorem 5.3.1 to the trivial group, or derive the exact equivalence of sheaf categories established in §5.1. A merely free action without the local slice condition does not supply this argument.','free action|descent|quotient cohomology')
add('P205-0002','comparison claims partly deferred','§5.3, Remark: canonical and Cartan resolutions','The canonical resolution computes the first and second equivariant spectral sequences together; the Cartan-resolution comparison under paracompactness is asserted without proof.','Build a comparison of double complexes compatible with both filtrations, not just their abutment; use the canonical product-resolution properties for the first claim. For a Cartan resolution verify the tensor exactness, support/fineness and equivariant comparison needed to identify the first filtration. The text explicitly does not use the latter assertion later.','canonical resolution|Cartan resolution|equivariant spectral sequence')
